==English==

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|rival|ry}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹaɪ.vəl.ɹi/|a=US,UK}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-rivalry.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# An [[ongoing]] [[relationship]] between (usually two) [[rival]]s who [[compete]] for [[superiority]].
#: {{ux|en|The Boston Bruins have a longstanding '''rivalry''' with the Montreal Canadiens.}}
#: {{ux|en|James and Polly have a bitter '''rivalry''' due to the latter's inclusion in the Tea Time Teaser.}}
# {{lb|en|economics}} The characteristic of being a [[rivalrous]] good, such that it can be consumed or used by only one person at a time.
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Department of Economics, University of Pittsburgh, at Johnstown|title=Public Goods|url=http://www.pitt.edu/~upjecon/MCG/MICRO/GOVT/Pubgood.html|accessdate=2013-12-02|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20150505060934/http://www.pitt.edu/~upjecon/MCG/MICRO/GOVT/Pubgood.html|archivedate=5 May 2015}}
#: {{ux|en|Common goods are those which are '''rivalrous''' in consumption but non-excludable}}
# Any [[competition]] between two or more things or factors.
#: {{ux|en|Binocular '''rivalry''' is a visual phenomenon in which perception alternates between different images presented to each eye.}}

